{"abstract":"Carbon footprint calculators (CFCs) have become widely referenced tools in the consumer-focussed green transition in Western societies. They are evoked to help measure current carbon emissions, establish a baseline, set reduction targets and monitor progress to reach them. While CFCs may be used in a wide range of settings, this chapter focusses on the CFCs that target individuals or households to trigger changes in consumption patterns and reduce carbon emissions. The empirical data on which the discussion is based consists of the testing of 30 different CFCs, mainly online or in apps, and on a survey comparing the carbon footprint of members of green communities in Denmark to that of a representative sampling of Danish citizens. The chapter first discusses the challenges of calculating carbon footprints. It then elicits implicit psychological and economic assumptions and theories used to design CFCs and the tendency to locate the key to behaviour change within individuals. Finally, it argues that CFCs could trigger wider environmental behaviour change if they were designed to measure the average consumption of communities that pursue green ideals.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Gausset, Quentin"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-809627"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"carbon footprint calculators and behaviour change","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-809627","identifier":[{"id":"105003360218","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-4440326c-5893-42aa-b451-c8b33775b9e4"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-AA29D967B00C2DA3867EE8FE35F7CCD0"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Anthropology, Department of Anthropology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Quentin","key":"gaussetpau","last":"Gausset","locid":"e6eb1072-50ff-40de-8ac8-d8e8cc694923","name":"Gausset, Quentin","orcid":"0000-0001-9826-3674","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Steffen","key":"dalsgaardpedh","last":"Dalsgaard","name":"Dalsgaard, Steffen","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Andy","key":"lautruppedh","last":"Lautrup","name":"Lautrup, Andy","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Katinka A.","key":"schybergpedh","last":"Schyberg","name":"Schyberg, Katinka A.","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Ingmar","key":"lippertpedh","last":"Lippert","name":"Lippert, Ingmar","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4324/9781003478669-3","eisbn":["9781040330951"],"isbn":["9781032764856"],"pages":"46-64","place":"London","publisher":"Taylor and Francis/Routledge","status":"p","subtitle":"Green Transformations in Contemporary Society","title":"The Cultural Complexity of Carbon"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105003360218"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-4440326c-5893-42aa-b451-c8b33775b9e4"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Carbon footprint calculators and behaviour change","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-809627","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"This volume discusses the transformational role that carbon – both as a concept and as a distinct set of material forms and effects – has come to play in social and cultural life. As a proxy for greenhouse gas emission data, carbon has grown to become a phenomenon that can no longer be accounted for solely within the technoscientific vocabulary of climate scientists. The Cultural Complexity of Carbon examines the extent to which our knowledge of carbon affects the way that human beings relate to each other and to the climate and/or the environment. It draws on case studies from a diverse range of topics including peatland restoration, religion and energy systems to explore questions that have so far been under-explored in the current literature. These questions include whether the recognition of carbon’s role in climate change leads to an incremental adaptation of lifestyles or to cultural or existential transformations, but also more concretely how carbon is made meaningful, and how these meanings are attached to ideals of cultural change or continuity. Spanning multiple perspectives and disciplinary positions, this volume provides a go-to point for the next generation of ethnographic studies of carbon and climate change. It cuts across what has hitherto been largely separate literatures in anthropology, geography and sociology to provide a meta-level orientation to how contemporary narratives of the role of carbon are being told. By addressing the intimate social and cultural changes that stem from humanity’s involvement with its natural and climatic resources, this volume is of interest to students and scholars of climate change within the social sciences and environmental humanities.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Dalsgaard, Steffen","Lautrup, Andy","Lippert, Ingmar","Schyberg, Katinka Amalie"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-818245"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Goethe University Frankfurt"],"keyword":["Built environment","Environment and Sustainability","Social sciences"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the cultural complexity of carbon","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"db","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.taylorfrancis.com/books/edit/10.4324/9781003478669/cultural-complexity-carbon-steffen-dalsgaard-andy-lautrup-ingmar-lippert-katinka-schyberg","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-818245","ids":["itu-7fd3f908-8982-4110-aaa6-081fdc6c99f0"],"keyword":["Built Environment","Environment and Sustainability","Social Sciences"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-34C1AAFA34993C5FAA1EA54B4CED51F6"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Climate IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"goethe university frankfurt","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Goethe University Frankfurt","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Steffen","key":"dalsgaardpau","last":"Dalsgaard","locid":"bc7a60f7-45a0-401f-813f-6b4aa700c849","name":"Dalsgaard, Steffen","orcid":"0000-0003-3712-6988","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andy","key":"lautruppau","last":"Lautrup","name":"Lautrup, Andy","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Ingmar","key":"lippertpau","last":"Lippert","name":"Lippert, Ingmar","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Katinka Amalie","key":"schybergpau","last":"Schyberg","name":"Schyberg, Katinka Amalie","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9781003478669"],"pages":"209","publisher":"Routledge","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-7fd3f908-8982-4110-aaa6-081fdc6c99f0"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Cultural Complexity of Carbon","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-818245","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"To address climate change, governments and corporations are increasingly turning to carbon dioxide removal (CDR). This invites us to revisit the concept of carbon accounting, as new carbon subjects emerge, being positioned by surrounding society as both “guilty” individuals and potential carbon removal providers. Employing the concepts of carbon subjects and user configuration, this chapter explores how Danish farmers are configured as “carbon farmers” through the introduction of biochar as a carbon removal method in Denmark. Based on participatory observation, field visits and semi-structured interviews, it interrogates how farmers imagine and are imagined engaging with biochar. It suggests that the configurations of the carbon farmers require both new farming and carbon accounting practices. Due to a narrow focus on technology, attention to new farming practices is often neglected or postponed, while emerging carbon accounting practices generate diverging expectations about the allocation of carbon removal. As a result, removed carbon may be claimed twice, which makes climate mitigation efforts seem larger than they are. I argue that biochar serves as a “technical adjustment,” where actors in and around the agricultural sector demonstrate willingness to take on new roles and practices to make the overall agro-industrial production model stay the same.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Hougaard, Inge-Merete"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-808993"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"configuring the carbon farmer emerging practices of carbon accounting and biochar engagements in danish agriculture","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-808993","identifier":[{"id":"105003359234","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-04511103-a428-4cae-95b8-5c1689298e41"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-4-2BDF33A350C2F69A980BFAA4BD296B43"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Anthropology, Department of Anthropology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Inge-Merete","key":"hougaardpau","last":"Hougaard","locid":"80d9c494-2fab-4cea-9a9b-5bd1ddebf3c5","name":"Hougaard, Inge-Merete","orcid":"0000-0001-7665-6522","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Steffen","key":"dalsgaardpedh","last":"Dalsgaard","name":"Dalsgaard, Steffen","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Andy","key":"lautruppedh","last":"Lautrup","name":"Lautrup, Andy","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Katinka","key":"schybergpedh","last":"Schyberg","name":"Schyberg, Katinka","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Ingmar","key":"lippertpedh","last":"Lippert","name":"Lippert, Ingmar","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4324/9781003478669","pages":"65-85","place":"London","publisher":"Routledge","status":"p","subtitle":"Green Transformations in Contemporary Society","title":"The Cultural Complexity of Carbon"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105003355877"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-04511103-a428-4cae-95b8-5c1689298e41"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Configuring the carbon farmer : Emerging practices of carbon accounting and biochar engagements in Danish agriculture","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-808993","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
