-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Arts, School of Culture and Society, School of Culture and Society - Department of Anthropology","Aarhus University, Interfaculty Centre, Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Steffen","key":"dalsgaardpau","last":"Dalsgaard","locid":"bc7a60f7-45a0-401f-813f-6b4aa700c849","name":"Dalsgaard, Steffen","orcid":"0000-0003-3712-6988","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Ton","key":"ottopau","last":"Otto","locid":"90fe48d1-ca66-4f68-81e3-fd48f0fa67b6","name":"Otto, Ton","orcid":"0000-0002-5185-3017","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1353/cp.2020.0043","issn":["1043898X","15279464"],"issn_org":["1043898X"],"issue":"2","journal":"The Contemporary Pacific","pages":"503-511","paperid":"774143","publisher":"University of Hawaii Press","status":"p","title":"The Contemporary Pacific","volume":"32"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85097365825"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-51306fea-72a6-445e-b071-8581f1fbfb77","itu-0e634db0-eb17-49db-a3ef-bc866bbc48b3"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"From Drifters to Asylum Seekers","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-018594","year":"2020","yearsub":2021}
{"abstract":"For almost two decades, Nauru and Papua New Guinea’s Manus Island have been central to the Australian government’s efforts to dispose of unwanted asylum seekers trying to reach Australian shores and to deter future asylum seekers from even attempting to obtain humanitarian protection. This policy, sometimes called the “Pacific Solution,” has created challenges for local Pacific populations and has placed these two Pacific Islands in the center of a geopolitical humanitarian crisis. The rhetoric surrounding the role of Nauru and Manus often positions their contemporary dilemmas within a framework of continued imperialist or neocolonial challenges to their sovereignty by their Australian neighbor. But it also does much more. The essays in this dialogue section interrogate the Pacific Solution and surrounding discourses by exploring the critical circumstances enveloping the two islands, as well as the movement of refugees in the Pacific more generally. This draws attention both to international conflict and to climate change and the resulting environmental calamities in the Pacific region. Other contributions interrogate refugee policy through ethnographic studies of the encounters between refugees and host populations, revealing the pressure felt by local Pacific populations and the responses available to them under the current circumstances. Some of these responses exceed scholarship and demand narrative art (Kaiku; Sparks-Ngenge), while others involve political dynamics that are entangled in responses to climate change (Bino) or in colonial histories (Dalsgaard and Otto; Kanngieser), as well as their logics and legal articulations (Keenan). The responses reveal issues of inclusion/exclusion denoting different sorts of “insiders” but also perspectives that require attention to intimacies and lived experience (Salyer; West).","author_count":3,"contributors":["Salyer, John C.","Dalsgaard, Steffen","West, Paige"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382018"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Banard College","Columbia University"],"keyword":["Asylum seekers","Australia","Manus","Nauru","Pacific Solution","Refugees"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"it is not because they are bad people australia s refugee resettlement in papua new guinea and nauru","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85588581/d1_Salyer_Dalsgaard_West.docx","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382018","identifier":[{"id":"85097388059","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-336401fd-5976-4f63-9f71-a04d71d04fa8"],"keyword":["Australia","Manus","Nauru","Pacific Solution","asylum seekers","refugees"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-8239BDA32D350C82F9631553C2053F6D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85588581/d1_Salyer_Dalsgaard_West.docx","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"banard college","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Banard College","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"columbia university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Columbia University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"John C.","key":"salyerpau","last":"Salyer","name":"Salyer, John C.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Steffen","key":"dalsgaardpau","last":"Dalsgaard","locid":"bc7a60f7-45a0-401f-813f-6b4aa700c849","name":"Dalsgaard, Steffen","orcid":"0000-0003-3712-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Paige","key":"westpau","last":"West","name":"West, Paige","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1353/cp.2020.0036","issn":["1043898X","15279464"],"issn_org":["1043898X"],"issue":"2","journal":"The Contemporary Pacific","pages":"435-448","publisher":"University of Hawaii Press","status":"p","title":"The Contemporary Pacific","volume":"32"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-336401fd-5976-4f63-9f71-a04d71d04fa8"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"“It Is Not Because They Are Bad People”: Australia’s Refugee Resettlement in Papua New Guinea and Nauru","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382018","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
