{"abstract":"Anthropological definitions and demarcations of ‘the field’ remain fundamentally anchored in tropes of location and spatiality, and the association between field and fieldworker is still primarily characterised as being maintained by distance in space. This article argues that ‘the field’ must be regarded as much as temporally constituted as it is normally seen as spatial. By exploring and unfolding the temporal properties of the field (e.g. different tempos, paces, extensions and projections of past, future etc.), it is suggested that the spatially anchored notion of multi-sited fieldwork can be complemented and extended with one of multi-temporal ethnography.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Dalsgaard, Steffen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383017"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Anthropology","Fieldwork Temporal Properties","Multi sited Fieldwork","Spatiality","Temporal Ethnography"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the field as a temporal entity and the challenges of the contemporary","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84879484970","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383017","identifier":[{"id":"84879484970","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-76a90fc0-a1c8-4581-bed8-a0404db0164c"],"keyword":["Anthropology","Fieldwork Temporal Properties","Multi-sited Fieldwork","Spatiality","Temporal Ethnography"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-43EEC65CEE2291F9731A780AE4C3102D"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2013","yearSub":"2013"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice","The Department, Culture, Aesthetics, Organisations and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Steffen","key":"dalsgaardpau","last":"Dalsgaard","locid":"bc7a60f7-45a0-401f-813f-6b4aa700c849","name":"Dalsgaard, Steffen","orcid":"0000-0003-3712-6988","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["09640282","14698676"],"issn_org":["09640282"],"issue":"2","journal":"Social Anthropology","pages":"213-225","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell","status":"p","title":"Social Anthropology","volume":"21"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84879484970"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-76a90fc0-a1c8-4581-bed8-a0404db0164c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Field as a Temporal Entity and the Challenges of the Contemporary","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383017","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"New transiting planet candidates are identified in 16 months (2009 May-2010 September) of data from the Kepler spacecraft. Nearly 5000 periodic transit-like signals are vetted against astrophysical and instrumental false positives yielding 1108 viable new planet candidates, bringing the total count up to over 2300. Improved vetting metrics are employed, contributing to higher catalog reliability. Most notable is the noise-weighted robust averaging of multi-quarter photo-center offsets derived from difference image analysis that identifies likely background eclipsing binaries. Twenty-two months of photometry are used for the purpose of characterizing each of the candidates. Ephemerides (transit epoch, T 0, and orbital period, P) are tabulated as well as the products of light curve modeling: reduced radius (R P/R sstarf), reduced semimajor axis (d/R sstarf), and impact parameter (b). The largest fractional increases are seen for the smallest planet candidates (201% for candidates smaller than 2 R ⊕ compared to 53% for candidates larger than 2 R ⊕) and those at longer orbital periods (124% for candidates outside of 50 day orbits versus 86% for candidates inside of 50 day orbits). The gains are larger than expected from increasing the observing window from 13 months (Quarters 1-5) to 16 months (Quarters 1-6) even in regions of parameter space where one would have expected the previous catalogs to be complete. Analyses of planet frequencies based on previous catalogs will be affected by such incompleteness. The fraction of all planet candidate host stars with multiple candidates has grown from 17% to 20%, and the paucity of short-period giant planets in multiple systems is still evident. The progression toward smaller planets at longer orbital periods with each new catalog release suggests that Earth-size planets in the habitable zone are forthcoming if, indeed, such planets are abundant.","author_count":76,"contributors":["Batalha, Natalie M.","Rowe, Jason F.","Bryson, Stephen T.","Barclay, Thomas","Burke, Christopher J.","Caldwell, Douglas A.","Christiansen, Jessie L.","Mullally, Fergal","Thompson, Susan E.","Brown, Timothy M.","Dupree, Andrea K.","Fabrycky, Daniel C.","Ford, Eric B.","Fortney, Jonathan J.","Gilliland, Ronald L.","Isaacson, Howard","Latham, David W.","Marcy, Geoffrey W.","Quinn, Samuel N.","Ragozzine, Darin","Shporer, Avi","Borucki, William J.","Ciardi, David R.","Gautier, Thomas N.","Haas, Michael R.","Jenkins, Jon M.","Koch, David G.","Lissauer, Jack J.","Rapin, William","Basri, Gibor S.","Boss, Alan P.","Buchhave, Lars A.","Carter, Joshua A.","Charbonneau, David","Christensen-Dalsgaard, Jørgen","Clarke, Bruce D.","Cochran, William D.","Demory, Brice-Olivier","Desert, Jean-Michel","Devore, Edna","Doyle, Laurance R.","Esquerdo, Gilbert A.","Everett, Mark","Fressin, Francois","Geary, John C.","Girouard, Forrest R.","Gould, Alan","Hall, Jennifer R.","Holman, Matthew J.","Howard, Andrew W.","Howell, Steve B.","Ibrahim, Khadeejah A.","Kinemuchi, Karen","Kjeldsen, Hans","Klaus, Todd C.","Li, Jie","Lucas, Philip W.","Meibom, Søren","Morris, Robert L.","Prša, Andrej","Quintana, Elisa","Sanderfer, Dwight T.","Sasselov, Dimitar","Seader, Shawn E.","Smith, Jeffrey C.","Steffen, Jason H.","Still, Martin","Stumpe, Martin C.","Tarter, Jill C.","Tenenbaum, Peter","Torres, Guillermo","Twicken, Joseph D.","Uddin, Kamal","van Cleve, Jeffrey","Walkowicz, Lucianne","Welsh, William F."],"cuid":["S02-100.00-025844"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"51-200 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Ames Research Center","California Institute of Technology","Carnegie Science","Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT)","Pennsylvania State University","Princeton University","SETI Institute","San Diego State University","San Jose State University","Smithsonian Institution","Univer