null,"first":"P.","key":"tenenbaumpau","last":"Tenenbaum","name":"Tenenbaum, P.","rank":60,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"S.E.","key":"thompsonpau","last":"Thompson","name":"Thompson, S.E.","rank":61,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"G.","key":"torrespau","last":"Torres","name":"Torres, G.","rank":62,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"J.D.","key":"twickenpau","last":"Twicken","name":"Twicken, J.D.","rank":63,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"18","corresponding":null,"first":"W.F.","key":"welshpau","last":"Welsh","name":"Welsh, W.F.","rank":64,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"20","corresponding":null,"first":"J.N.","key":"winnpau","last":"Winn","name":"Winn, J.N.","rank":65,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["23599262"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1126/science.1234702","issn":["00368075","10959203"],"issn_org":["00368075"],"issue":"6132","journal":"Science","pages":"587-590","publisher":"American Association for the Advancement of Science","status":"p","title":"Science","volume":"340"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84877325672"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-f4b195d9-b829-46f9-a51e-ac17e6c05dfa","ku-4b27de8a-cd6b-45b5-a622-63da3600bd78"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Kepler-62 : A five-planet system with planets of 1.4 and 1.6 Earth radii in the habitable zone","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.46-030321","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"Prompted by the postmodern turn in anthropology, ethnographic fieldwork has been subjected to considerable analytical scrutiny. Yet despite numerous conceptual facelifts, definitions and demarcations of 'the field' have remained fundamentally anchored in tropes of spatiality with the association between field and fieldworker characterized as being maintained by distances in space. By exploring and unfolding the temporal properties of the field, anthropology can favorably complement and extend the (spatially anchored) notion of multi-sited fieldwork with one of multi-temporal ethnography. This approach implies not only a particular attention to the methodology of studying local (social and ontological) imaginaries of time; it furthermore unpacks the (multi-)temporality of the relationship between fieldworker and the field. This special issue may thus be taken as a fresh invitation to a temporally oriented ethnography.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Dalsgaard, Steffen","Nielsen, Morten"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382093"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Ethnography","Method","Multi sited ethnography","Temporality","Time","multi temporality","ﬁeldwork"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"introduction time and the field","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-382093","ids":["itu-37b9d7a8-3b8e-4241-ab84-eee843127d10"],"keyword":["ethnography","method","multi-sited ethnography","multi-temporality","temporality","time","ﬁeldwork"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-333B341A26429885F68E772FDE9AE357"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"